<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Husayn Bayqara</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 1469–1506</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Husain Bayqara</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Husayn Bayqara was a Timurid ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> who reigned from 1469 to 1506. His court was a brilliant centre of Persian and Chagatai literature, painting and architecture, with figures such as the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-shir-navai">Ali-Shir Nava'i</a> and the painter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamal-ud-din-behzad">Bihzad</a>. His reign marked the cultural high point of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/husayn-bayqara.jpg" alt="Husayn Bayqara" width="262" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kamāl ud-Dīn Behzād · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Sultan_Husayn_Mirza_Bayqara_at_the_age_of_about_50_years._1500%E2%80%9325_copy_from_Behz%C4%81d%27s_original_of_about_1490.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
